Rob Ellwood <rob.ellwood@shaw.ca> writes:

[bug whereby a shop annexes an unrelated area of Bustling Town caves
to consider part of its premises.]

> 	It would be nice if we could nail down the symptoms of
> the bug and develop a workaround.  Suggestions if you get the 
> Bustling Town level:
> 
> 	- Watch for a shopkeeper's "welcome!" message when you 
> aren't in a shop.  Dunno if it shows up every time, but it did
> show up at least once.  

It's the only way I'm currently looking for the bug, so I can't really
answer that :-) But you always ought to, if my understanding of the bug's
mechanics is correct.

(Summary: creating a cavernous level involves assigning patches of
space roomno values during the process of making sure they're all
connected. This would cause confusion if a shop is later assigned the
same roomno as one that's been used as a cavern label and not removed,
since the roomno of a location is how the game checks to see if you're
in a shop. There's code that's meant to ensure this Doesn't Happen,
but it looks to be failing in a small number of cases (or, the number
of cases is small to start with and the failure rate is higher; my
comprehension of the way this can happen, as opposed to the results
when it does, is sadly fuzzy).)

> 	- If you get the message, try dropping money right
> where you are.  See if you get a store credit.

You do.

> 	- Go back to the Official Area spot.  Drop an object which 
> this particular shopkeeper never buys.  (Or, if it's a general store,
> something you don't mind losing.)  Do you get an appropriate message?  

  "Hello, wizard!  Welcome again to Izchak's lighting store!"
  You drop an uncursed lock pick.  It seems uninterested.

  You drop a candle.
  It offers 5 gold pieces for your candle.  Sell it? [ynaq] (y)
  You sold a candle for 5 gold pieces.

> 	- While standing over the object, try #chatting with the
> shopkeeper.  Do you get an appropriate message?  [I think that you
> would only get messages if the shopkeeper bought what you dropped,
> *unless* the shopkeeper owns dropped objects, even though he hasn't 
> paid for them.]

  Fine goods for sale:
  
  a candle, 19 zorkmids
  an uncursed lock pick, no charge

> 	- If you're brave or buff, try picking up the object.  Do
> the Kops show up, or are you charged for the object?

  "For you, most gracious sir; only 19 for this candle."
  j - a candle (unpaid, 19 zorkmids).
  [p]
  There appears to be no shopkeeper here to receive your payment.  
  [move out]
  Your credit of 100 zorkmids is used to cover your shopping bill.

(Yes, all of it. This is the usual practice, sharp though it is.)

> 	- Move back and forth.  Try to chart out the Official Area.
> How big is it?  The same size as the shop, or half the MAP, or what?  
> If we can nail down the problem area, we'll be a lot safer.

It's the same size as whatever patch-of-space was used during level
creation to create that part of the cavern; it'll have irregular
walls, but it'll be something you can point to and say "yeah, that's
all one area of cave". It needn't be any particular size, as far as I
can tell. (And it's bounded by the walls; no wall or rock areas being
considered shop.)

> 	- If possible, throw something of low value into the 
> Official Area from outside the Area.  Go to the object and #chat.
> If the shopkeeper tells you the price, people can then test each
> object and corpse to see if it's in the Official Area.  

You'll always be told the price; this doesn't depend on LOS to
the shopkeeper. (The only thing that does is payment, I think.)

> 	Dylan:  about how many tests did it take you to reproduce 
> the bug?  

First time: First one I tried. Then not again for seven or eight
tries. (A good order, otherwise I'd have written it off after half a
dozen as irreproducible.) Today, came up on the tenth try.

> 	And I'm afraid that I find "entering any square off the 
> east edge of the predefined map" ambiguous.  I believe that you mean
> "exiting the MAP along the east edge" instead of "entering the MAP
> from the east".

I meant "entering any square that was beyond the east edge of the
MAP"; entering by any means, whether that's walking from an adjacent
MAP square, teleporting, coming up the stairs... the game doesn't care
how you arrive in a shop, just whether you're not there one turn and
are the next. (The "one cave" in that case was a big one. In the
case that came up today, a smaller one. Also on the east side, though,
which probably has some relation to the roomno ordering.)

